    "evidence_text": "The Bile Acid Chenodeoxycholic Acid Increases Human Brown Adipose Tissue Activity.\n\nWhole-body energy expenditure was also increased upon CDCA treatment.\n\nThese findings identify bile acids as a target to activate BAT in humans.\n\nAcetic acid consumed a large amount of ATP, resulting in an increase in AMPK\u03b1 phosphorylation.\n\nConsequently, the triglyceride content in the acetate-treated hepatocytes was significantly decreased.\n\nEffect of a C/EBP gene replacement on mitochondrial biogenesis in fat cells.\n\nEnergy expenditure was greater in absolute terms in the obese women.\n\nSleeping energy expenditure was particularly low in the post-obese group when high-fat fed.\n\nIncreased resting energy expenditure and weight loss are related to a systemic inflammatory response in lung cancer patients.\n\n12%; 67 patients were considered hypermetabolic.\n\nA switch from white to brown fat increases energy expenditure in cancer-associated cachexia.\n\nLimited therapeutic options are available and the underlying mechanisms are poorly defined.\n\nImportantly, UCP1 staining is observed in WAT from CAC patients.\n\nUncoupling Mitochondrial Respiration for Diabesity.\n\nThese drugs did not affect VO2 in control animals.\n\nBile acids: natural ligands for an orphan nuclear receptor.",
